<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Venetian Nauplia</h1><p class="st-meta">1388 CE – 1540 CE · capital Nauplia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Venetian-ruled port of Nauplia, called Napoli di Romania, a fortified base in the Argolid for a century and a half.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1388 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-of-enghien">Marie of Enghien</a>, heiress of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-argos-and-nauplia">Lordship of Argos and Nauplia</a>, sold her lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. The Byzantine despot Theodore I seized <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/argos">Argos</a> first, but returned it to Venice in 1394. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a>, which the Venetians called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Napoli</a> di <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Romania</a>, became the centre of Venetian rule in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Venetian officials governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a>, strengthening the walls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acronauplia">Acronauplia</a> and building the harbour fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourtzi">Bourtzi</a> in 1471. The town traded grain, wine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and hosted Greek, Albanian and Italian communities; Albanian soldiers (stradioti) served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/argos">Argos</a> was lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1463, leaving Nauplia an outpost surrounded by Ottoman territory.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Ottoman-Venetian war of 1537-1540, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> under the peace treaty of 1540. Venice returned in 1686 during the creation of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/venetian-morea">Venetian Morea</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1388 CE – 1540 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.32%;width:3.02%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1388 CE – 1540 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nauplia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Venetian, Greek, Albanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Napoli di Romania, Venetian Argos and Nauplia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1464&amp;focus=venetian-nauplia" aria-label="Venetian Nauplia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5250 826 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Venetian Nauplia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5570 1082L5570 1082M5570 1082L5293 831M5570 1082L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5570 1082h0M5293 831h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5570 1082h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5570 1082h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nauplia. <a href="/atlas?year=1464&amp;focus=venetian-nauplia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
